course: medicine
lesson: 22
title: Mum's vitamin and cod-liver oil
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A hundred years ago many city children had rickets: soft bones and bowed legs, because smoke hid the sun and their skin could not make vitamin D, and a spoon of cod-liver oil really helped them.
explain: Today most children who eat a varied diet get their vitamins from food, and a pill can't copy all the fibre, energy and thousands of helpful substances in real food: food first, pills second.
explain: But some gaps are real: in the UK the winter sun is too weak to make vitamin D in the skin, so health advice says everyone should think about a daily vitamin D supplement from autumn to spring.
explain: Adverts love words like «boost your immune system», but boost is a selling word, not science: a healthy immune system works well with good food, sleep and exercise.
fact: Many children who eat a varied diet get the vitamins they need from food.
fact: UK advice is that children from 6 months to 5 years should have vitamins A, C and D, and everyone should consider 10 micrograms of vitamin D a day in autumn and winter.
fact: In the UK, sunlight from about October to March is not strong enough for the skin to make vitamin D.
fact: Rickets is a bone disease caused by lack of vitamin D, making bones soft and legs bowed; it was common in smoky cities and is still occasionally seen.
fact: Cod-liver oil contains omega-3 fats and vitamins A and D, and was used in the past to prevent rickets.
fact: Too much vitamin A can be harmful, so cod-liver oil should not be doubled up with other vitamin A supplements.
fact: Studies of fish oil supplements in healthy children have not shown clear improvements in intelligence or school results.
fact: Dietitians suggest eating oily fish, such as salmon or sardines, about once a week.
fact: Gummy vitamins often contain sugar and look like sweets, so they must be kept out of reach and only the dose on the packet taken; if a small child eats a lot of them, call 111 or a doctor at once.
fact: Very fussy eaters may miss some nutrients, so a doctor may suggest a multivitamin while the family widens the diet; fussy eating usually improves with age.
fact: Taking lots of supplements does not usually help healthy children and can sometimes cause harm.
fact: «Boosts your immune system» on an advert is a marketing phrase, not a proven medical claim.
fact: A multivitamin can't make up for a poor diet, because food gives fibre, energy and many substances a pill does not contain.
exercise: Do most children who eat a varied diet need lots of vitamin pills? | No, they usually get what they need from food | Yes, everyone needs five different vitamin pills a day | Yes, food has no vitamins now | Only on school days
exercise: Why does UK advice mention vitamin D tablets in winter? | Winter sunlight is too weak to make vitamin D in the skin | Vitamin D stops winter colds completely | There is no vitamin D in summer food | Snow blocks vitamin D
exercise: How much vitamin D does UK advice suggest in autumn and winter? | 10 micrograms a day | 10 grams a day | One spoonful of cod-liver oil an hour | 100 tablets a week
exercise: What is rickets? | Soft bones and bowed legs from lack of vitamin D | A cough caused by smoke | Bleeding gums and slow healing from lack of vitamin C | Tired blood from lack of iron
exercise: Why was rickets common in old smoky cities? | Smoke hid the sun so skin made little vitamin D | Children drank too much milk | Factories put iron dust in the air that softened bones | People ate too many carrots
exercise: Is rickets only a disease of the past? | No, it is still occasionally seen | Yes, it vanished for good back in 1900 | Yes, it only ever happened to kings and queens | No, nearly every child in Britain has it today
exercise: What does cod-liver oil contain? | Omega-3 fats and vitamins A and D | Vitamin C, fibre and a lot of sugar | Only calcium and iron, nothing else | Sugar and caffeine, like an energy drink
exercise: Why shouldn't cod-liver oil be doubled up with other vitamin A supplements? | Too much vitamin A can be harmful | It makes the oil taste worse | Vitamin A cancels vitamin D | It turns your skin orange at once
exercise: Do fish oil capsules make healthy children cleverer? | Studies haven't shown clear gains | Yes, by about ten IQ points every month | Yes, but only for maths and times tables | No, they actually make you more forgetful
exercise: What do dietitians suggest instead of brain pills? | Eating oily fish like salmon about once a week | Eating fish every meal | Avoiding fish altogether | Drinking fish oil by the cup with every single meal
exercise: Why must gummy vitamins be kept out of reach? | They look like sweets and children may eat too many | They melt in the light | They go off in a day | Grown-ups always secretly eat them all up themselves
exercise: What does «boosts your immune system» on an advert mean? | It is a marketing phrase, not a proven claim | The pill has been proven to stop all illness | The doctor wrote the advert | Your body needs it daily
exercise: Why do doctors say food first, pills second? | Food gives fibre, energy and much more that pills can't copy | Pills are always dangerous | Food has more vitamins than any pill in every case | Pills are only for grown-ups
exercise: Why might a fussy eater be given a multivitamin? | To fill gaps while the family widens his diet | To make him like broccoli | Because fussy eating never improves at all, whatever you do | To replace meals altogether
exercise: Does taking loads of supplements make a healthy child super healthy? | No, and sometimes it causes harm | Yes, the more pills you take, the better | Yes, as long as they are expensive ones | Only the gummy ones work like that
test: Grandma: "cod-liver oil every day or you'll get rickets like we did". What's true now? | Rickets came from lack of vitamin D, and today a winter vitamin D tablet or the right food does the job | Rickets came from not eating fish | Rickets can't happen any more | Cod-liver oil cures every illness
test: Advert: "Super-Kid Gummies BOOST immunity!" What should a smart kid ask? | What's the evidence? Boost is a selling word | How many can I eat at once? | Are they stronger than the medicine from the doctor? | Why don't doctors sell them?
test: "If I take a vitamin I can skip veg, right?" | No, a pill can't replace fibre and everything else in veg | Yes, the pill has everything | Yes, if it's a gummy | No, because veg has no vitamins
test: what happens if a toddler finds a jar of gummy vitamins and eats them all? | It can be dangerous, call 111 or a doctor at once | Nothing, they're just sweets | He'll get super healthy | He'll only get a little tummy ache, so no need to tell anyone
test: Liam swears fish oil made him top in spelling. Best reply? | Studies don't show fish oil makes healthy kids cleverer | Fish oil is proven to make every kid top of the whole class | It only works for spelling | Spelling comes from carrots
test: Why might a child who stays indoors in a dark northern winter need vitamin D more than one in sunny Spain in summer? | Strong sunlight lets skin make vitamin D, weak winter light doesn't | Spain puts vitamin D in the sea | Children in the north eat no food | Cold weather destroys bones
test: Sam's mum gives him eight different supplements a day. Is that wise? | Not usually, it rarely helps and can harm | Yes, more is always better | Yes, if he's under 10 | No, because supplements are illegal for children
test: Cousin eats only beige food. What did the doctor most likely do? | Suggested a multivitamin and tiny tastes of new food | Banned all food but beige | Gave him an operation | Said fussy eaters stay fussy forever, so there's nothing to do
test: A 3-year-old in the UK. What does health advice say about vitamins? | Daily vitamins A, C and D are recommended | No vitamins until age 10 | Only cod-liver oil | Fish oil every day to boost his brain power
test: In a game, eating a gold apple gives "+50 immunity". What's true in real life? | No food or pill gives an instant immunity boost | Gold apples work | Any apple gives +50 | Only pills give immunity points
test: Mum takes cod-liver oil and a multivitamin with vitamin A. What's the worry? | Doubling up vitamin A can be harmful | Nothing, vitamins never hurt anyone at all | The oil cancels out the pill completely | Fish oil and pills explode together in the tummy
exam: Ascertain the cause of the bone disease rickets, as recorded in the urban populations of former centuries. | A deficiency of vitamin D, owing to insufficient sunlight | An excess of dietary calcium | A deficiency of vitamin C, owing to a want of fresh fruit and vegetables | An infection transmitted by fish
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the supplementation of omega-3 oils in healthy children. | No clear improvement in intelligence or attainment has been demonstrated | A substantial improvement in intelligence has been demonstrated | Supplementation is compulsory under statute | Such oils are injurious in any quantity
exam: Pursuant to the health advice issued in the United Kingdom, determine the supplement which all persons are advised to consider during the autumn and winter months. | Vitamin D, ten micrograms daily | Vitamin C, ten grams upon each day | Iron, one tablet upon every hour | Cod-liver oil, one cup upon each day
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the expression «boosts the immune system» in commercial advertising warrants scepticism. | It is a marketing phrase and not an established medical claim | It is prohibited by medical law | It refers exclusively to adults | It denotes a prescription-only medicine
